      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "The distribution of the nerve action potentials reflexively elicited by electrical stimulation of the lumbar spine was investigated in rats."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To elucidate the relation between the lumbar spine and other body regions that compose the spinal reflex."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "The hypothesis was that the ventral portion of the L5-L6 disc spatially corresponds to the groin."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In Experiments 1 and 2, wire electrodes were placed 1) in the ventral and dorsal portions of the disc, facet joint, and muscle fascia at L5-L6, and 2) in the ventral portions of L3-L4, L4-L5, L5-L6, and L6-S discs. A needle electrode was inserted in the L5-L6 disc by 0.4-mm increments, and action potentials were serially recorded from the genitofemoral nerve."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Experiments 1 and 2: Reflex action potentials were elicited in the iliohypogastric (T13 and L1), ilioinguinal (L1), and genitofemoral (L2) nerves. Experiment 1: Stimulation of the disc induced reflex discharges significantly more frequently than stimulation of the facet joint and muscle fascia. Experiment 2: The more cranial the disc stimulated, the more frequently the reflex discharge was induced in the iliohypogastric nerve. Experiment 3: The depth of stimulation did not influence the size of the reflex action potential."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Electrical stimulation of the lumbar disc and facet joint induced reflex discharges in the nerves to the lower abdominal regions. It was postulated that the reflex discharges are related to muscle contraction resulting in referred pain in the loin and groin."
        }
      ]},
